WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.559 --> 00:00:05.830
The CW's crossover event is reaching out to
the live-action TV and film adaptations of

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:10.740
the past and present to weave as many characters
as possible into Crisis' rich tapestry.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:16.100
Heroes and villains are falling and rising,
worlds are dying, and the cameos flow freely.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.080
From here on in, be prepared for spoilers.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:25.080
Crisis on Infinite Earths doesn't waste any
time before it pays fan service to popular

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.980
DC superhero adaptations of old.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:32.780
Part One of the crossover opens with cameos
from across classic comic book films and TV,

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:37.860
beginning with Robert Wuhl reprising his role
as ambitious investigative reporter Alexander

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.380
Knox on Earth-89.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:44.670
Wuhl played Knox the only reporter in Gotham
who believed Batman was real and was determined

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:48.760
to find out who he was in Tim Burton's 1989
Batman movie.

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:50.880
"This is Pulitzer Prize stuff, guys"

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:56.340
We never learn if Knox's coverage of the Dark
Knight wins him the acclaim he always wanted,

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:00.530
but judging by the paper he's reading, Batman
coverage is at least a little more mainstream

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:01.300
these days.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:06.300
Among the myriad of different worlds we're
shown succumbing to the antimatter wave in

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.960
the opening minutes of Crisis on Infinite
Earths is Earth-9.

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:15.100
We see only two young heroes' faces before
the end comes, and if you're not familiar

00:01:15.109 --> 00:01:20.009
with the DC Universe streaming service's original
programming, you might not have any idea who

00:01:20.009 --> 00:01:20.500
they are.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.500
Both heroes hail from DC Universe's very first
original series, Titans.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:30.480
The hero in the red-and-white suit is Hawk
and the younger hero in the domino mask is

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:30.980
Robin.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:36.260
Specifically, this Robin is Jason Todd who,
in the comics, is the second boy to take on

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:40.300
the name Robin, eventually dying violently
at the hands of the Joker.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.899
Their appearance in Crisis confirms that Titans'
world exists within the same multiverse.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:49.220
Or, at least, it used to exist in the same
multiverse.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:56.100
The final cameo from Crisis Part One's cold
open is from one of DC Comics' earliest live-action

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:56.860
adaptations.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:02.450
Burt Ward, who played Robin alongside Adam
West in the '60s camp series Batman, is walking

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:06.660
his dog in Gotham City as the antimatter wave
hits Earth-66.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:11.180
While he doesn't appear to be crimefighting
anymore, the neckline on Ward's shirt has

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:15.600
a black-yellow-green pattern reminiscent of
Robin's old school costume.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:19.680
And if that, along with the signature Batman
theme song, aren't enough to let you know

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:23.760
exactly who this is, his single line of dialogue
should do the trick.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:26.580
"Holy crimson skies of death!"

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:33.099
One of the more fun surprises in Crisis is
the return of Jon Cryer as Superman's arch-nemesis,

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:34.340
billionaire Lex Luthor.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:39.180
By the end of Crisis Part Three he's stolen
the Book of Destiny twice and tried to murder

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:40.959
multiple versions of Superman.

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:46.319
He eventually succeeds in murdering the Superman
of Earth-96, replacing him as the Paragon

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:46.980
of Truth.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:51.300
The return of Cryer's Lex Luthor is noteworthy
for a number of reasons.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:56.080
Not only is Cryer perfect in the role, but
he's a DC adaptation alumnus.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:02.580
Cryer plays Lex Luthor's annoying nephew Lenny
in the 1987 bomb Superman IV: The Quest for

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:03.220
Peace.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:08.320
In the Crisis Aftermath after-show following
Crisis Part Three, Cryer told Kevin Smith

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.100
he saw his casting as Luthor as a chance to
redeem that earlier flop.

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:14.740
"Yeah you're just an experiment, freako.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:18.300
What is that?

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:20.180
Oh noooo."

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:25.760
The Monitor, played by LaMonica Garrett, is
a character lifted directly from the original

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:28.340
DC Comics event Crisis on Infinite Earths.

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:33.500
When the multiverse was created, most universes
were comprised of positive matter, but there

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:35.560
was one made of anti-matter.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:41.300
The Monitor is a living, breathing avatar
for the positive matter in the universe, while

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:45.000
the Anti-Monitor is his opposite in the anti-matter
universe.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.840
Each is instantly aware of the other, and
they battle for millennia.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:54.160
Just as it happens in the TV event, the comics
crossover sees the Monitor recruit superheroes

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:59.319
to help, and just like the end of Part Three
of CW's Crisis, in the comic it's a corrupted

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:02.520
version of Harbinger that kills the godlike
being.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.140
The character Harbinger is proof
that the creative teams behind the Arrowverse

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:10.720
have had Crisis in the back of their minds
for longer than you might realize.

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:15.600
Audrey Marie Anderson has played Lyla Michaels
on Arrow since 2013.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:20.989
She originally shows up as John Diggle's ex-wife,
and she eventually replaces Amanda Waller

00:04:20.989 --> 00:04:23.620
as the head of the clandestine group A.R.G.U.S.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:28.900
John Diggle and Lyla reunite and have a son
- well it was a daughter at first but then

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:33.139
Barry time traveled and their daughter became
a son… it’s complicated, and with the

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:38.080
coming of Crisis, Lyla is transformed into
the Monitor's protege Harbinger.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:42.680
Fans of the comics have probably expected
something like this was coming for a while.

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:48.380
In the main Crisis story, Lyla Michaels becomes
Harbinger just about immediately, and works

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:51.060
with the godlike being to prepare for the
Crisis.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:57.020
Just as it happens in the CW event, in the
comics Harbinger is corrupted by the Anti-Monitor

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:58.840
and used to kill the Monitor.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:04.860
After Lex Luthor gets his hands on the Book
of Destiny in Crisis Part Two, he decides

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:08.160
to wipe out every Superman in every reality.

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:14.000
The vengeful quest brings him to man chopping
wood at a farm who has no S on his chest and

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.220
little patience for Lex.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.880
In case you couldn't tell by the flannel,
the man is Clark Kent, played by Tom Welling,

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:21.900
from the hit series Smallville.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:26.440
In Crisis, Part Two, we learn that since the
conclusion of Smallville Clark has given up

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:28.760
his powers and settled in to a simpler life.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:34.370
When Jon Cryer's Lex Luthor travels to Earth-167
to deal with Kent, the revelation that this

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:38.580
Superman gave up his powers makes him furious
with confusion and disbelief.

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:41.400
"You gave up your powers?”

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:42.750
“Can't say I’ve missed these chats."

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:49.420
As soon as images began emerging of Brandon
Routh as the Superman of Earth-96 in Crisis,

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:55.780
it was clear this take on Superman was heavily
inspired by the 1996 comic Kingdom Come.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:06:00.500
Routh's Superman outfit, particularly the
black included in the Superman insignia, is

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:02.300
very reminiscent of that version.

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:05.060
But of course this isn't just a call back
to a comic.

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:10.280
Brandon Routh was tapped to play Superman
even though he already has an Arrowverse role

00:06:10.289 --> 00:06:15.250
— as Earth-1's Atom on Legends of Tomorrow
— because the actor stepped into Supes'

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:18.240
red boots in 2006's Superman Returns.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:24.440
In Part 2 of CW's Crisis on Infinite Earths
crossover, Batwoman and Supergirl travel to

00:06:24.449 --> 00:06:29.770
Earth-99 to recruit who they believe is the
multiverse's Paragon of Courage: that Earth's

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:30.940
version of Batman.

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:35.520
"So, the Paragon of Courage is apparently
afraid of yard work.”

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:37.320
“Better let me do the talking."

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:43.180
Instead of a Paragon, the pair find a bitter,
angry Bruce Wayne who is a dark fusion of

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:45.020
different versions of the Dark Knight.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:50.069
Kevin Conroy, best known as the Batman of
The Animated Series, has given his voice to

00:06:50.069 --> 00:06:55.000
Gotham's protector in numerous animated TV,
film, and video game projects for the past

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:56.000
27 years.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.000
In Crisis Part Two, he physically portrays
the Dark Knight for the first time, although

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:02.460
it isn't the version we expected.

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:06.900
We learn that this version of Wayne would
eventually resort to murder to achieve his

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:09.780
goals, including killing his world's Superman.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:13.100
Before his death, Wayne tells Supergirl something
interesting:

00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:16.580
"My parents taught me a very different lesson.

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:19.380
Life only makes sense if you force it to."

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:27.040
The line comes almost word for word from the
1986 comic Batman: The Dark Knight Returns,

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:31.860
from Batman's narration as he fights Superman,
though even that darker realization of Bruce

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:33.820
Wayne doesn't seek to kill Clark.

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:40.080
Ever since Tom Cavanagh's original Flash character
Harrison Wells bit the dust, it seems we've

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:43.480
gotten new alternate Earth versions of Wells
every season.

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:48.820
This season of Flash begins with a version
of the character named Nash Wells, who has

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:53.640
a bit of an Indiana Jones flare, but that
changes when he unintentionally frees the

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:55.680
Anti-Monitor and causes the Crisis.

00:07:55.980 --> 00:08:01.500
As punishment, Wells is made into Pariah and
is forced to witness tragedy as it unfolds.

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:03.820
Things work a bit differently in the comics.

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:07.560
In the Crisis comics Pariah's real name is
Kell Mossa.

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:12.240
Kells conducts experiments allowing him to
see the birth of the universe, but in doing

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:16.680
so attracts the attention of the Anti-Monitor,
who destroys Mossa's Earth.

00:08:17.050 --> 00:08:21.740
The Monitor saves Pariah's life and gives
him the ability to travel from Earth to Earth,

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:23.080
witnessing as each is destroyed.

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:30.580
Crisis Part Three opens in New Gotham on Earth-203
with a hero running from the antimatter wave.

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:34.210
Unlike many of the multiverse cameos we've
seen in Crisis, this time we actually get

00:08:34.210 --> 00:08:36.880
to see as the wave hits the hero, killing
her.

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:43.300
The hero is Huntress, played by Ashley Scott,
from the 2002-03 TV series Birds of Prey.

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:44.920
"Who the hell are you?”

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:47.260
“I'm the Huntress, and you're the prey."

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:49.840
Huntress wasn't the only hero on the show.

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:54.980
Over her coms we hear the voice of Oracle,
formerly known as Batgirl, who becomes the

00:08:54.980 --> 00:08:59.800
intelligence arm of the Birds of Prey after
she's paralyzed by a bullet from the Joker.

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:05.880
In Crisis Part Three, Team Flash's Cisco Ramon
joins the efforts to find the multiverse's

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:12.500
Paragons, and his efforts lead them to a scientist
named Ryan Choi, the Paragon of Humanity.

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:17.480
He's unique among the Paragons in that he
isn't a superhero or supervillain, though

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:21.660
if the source material is any gauge, that
won't stay true for too long.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:28.140
In DC Comics, Ryan Choi first fills Ray Palmer's
place by taking his job at Ivy University

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:32.340
and later by taking on his abilities as the
new version of the shrinking hero Atom.

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:39.200
Choi's solo series All New Atom folded after
25 issues, though he remains Atom in the comics.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:45.080
He's eventually murdered by Deathstroke, but
after DC's line-wide New 52 reboot and the

00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:48.740
subsequent event Convergence, Choi returns
to the narrative.

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:53.660
He appeared in the most recent Justice League
of America series as the Atom, alongside an

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:58.080
eccentric line-up that included Batman, Lobo,
and Killer Frost.

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:04.160
Perhaps no one should have been shocked that
Crisis Part Three features a cameo by Tom

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:10.020
Ellis as Lucifer Morningstar, the lead of
Netflix's Lucifer and, yes, the devil himself.

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:15.360
After all, the world that John Constantine,
John Diggle, and Mia Smoak travel to is Earth-666.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:22.410
Netflix, DC Comics, and the CW largely have
Neil Gaiman to thank for this version of Lucifer.

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:27.160
The character first appears in Gaiman's classic
Sandman series as an adversary to its hero,

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:31.810
and later got a solo comic series Lucifer
which was then adapted into a TV series for

00:10:31.810 --> 00:10:32.300
Fox.

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:37.620
Lucifer was canceled by Fox after its third
season, but the series was picked up by Netflix.

00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:42.270
In June 2019 we learned that Lucifer had been
renewed for a fifth and final season on the

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:43.260
streaming service.

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:48.580
Crisis Part Three sees the return and sacrifice
of the Flash of Earth-90.

00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:54.040
Last year's CW crossover, Elseworlds, opened
with this version of the Flash being the sole

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:56.080
surviving hero on Earth-90.

00:10:56.320 --> 00:11:01.340
He soon joined the Arrowverse heroes to face
the Monitor, but disappeared early in the

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:02.600
crossover without a trace.

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:07.950
We now know where he was as a prisoner of
the Anti-Monitor, Earth-90 Flash was forced

00:11:07.950 --> 00:11:11.340
to run a treadmill that powered the antimatter
wave.

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:17.640
Toward the end of Crisis Part Three, he sacrifices
himself to stop the wave and save Earth-1.

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:20.320
This isn't just another alternate universe
Flash.

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:25.639
Played by John Wesley Shipp, this Flash is
the same hero who was the lead for the short-lived

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:28.430
The Flash series from 1990 to '91.

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:32.040
"Thanks, Flash.

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:34.980
At least someone around here knows how to
rescue a kid."

00:11:35.420 --> 00:11:39.430
CW's The Flash has never been lazy about paying
tribute to its predecessor.

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:44.060
Shipp has played multiple roles on The Flash,
including versions of the hero from other

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:44.560
worlds.

00:11:45.460 --> 00:11:50.000
Ever since Oliver Queen's death at the end
of Crisis Part One, the heroes have been searching

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:51.580
for a way to bring him back to life.

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:56.740
In Part Two they bring his body to a Lazarus
Pit on an alternate Earth, where they manage

00:11:56.740 --> 00:12:00.060
to revive Green Arrow's body, but not his
soul.

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:05.180
John Constantine does his best to bring back
Queen's soul, but in Part Three they're forced

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:07.700
to travel to Purgatory to retrieve it directly.

00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:13.320
After a tearful reunion it seems that Oliver
will return to the land of the living... until

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:16.140
a mysterious man named Jim Corrigan appears.

00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:20.280
If you're a DC Comics fan, then that name
means a lot.

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:25.520
Jim Corrigan is a former police detective
who takes on the role of the Spectre, a powerful

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:29.960
entity who in some incarnations is meant to
be the literal Wrath of God.

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:35.660
In the comics, Corrigan was eventually replaced
as the host for the Spectre by the Green Lantern

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:39.820
Hal Jordan, who had died in the 1996 event
Final Night.

00:12:40.089 --> 00:12:44.660
The fact that Corrigan appears when he does
and stops Oliver Queen from crossing over

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:49.660
back to the land of the living suggests that
it may now be Oliver Queen who is called upon

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:51.180
to be the host of the Spectre.

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:53.440
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:56.700
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
shows are coming soon.

00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:00.120
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

